\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Fix a prime $p$ and a positive chromatic height $n\ge1$. In the category of dualizable $K(n)$-local spectra at $p$, classify thick tensor ideals. A thick tensor ideal is closed under cofibers, retracts, and tensoring with any dualizable object. Does their inclusion order give the expected chromatic filtration analogous to the thick-subcategory theorem for finite spectra?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~tlawson/hovey/}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Fix a prime $p$ and a positive chromatic height $n\ge1$. In the category of dualizable $K(n)$-local spectra at $p$, classify thick tensor ideals. A thick tensor ideal is closed under cofibers, retracts, and tensoring with any dualizable object. Does their inclusion order give the expected chromatic filtration analogous to the thick-subcategory theorem for finite spectra?

